#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc5eed68-6762-5dde-b87b-876f84c8b4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376005} {:answer/id #uuid "cf907ddc-0b96-5684-9d00-0b32e2f5b341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-6t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376006} {:answer/id #uuid "805449a0-a536-5725-bc89-cc35ec3b7c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376007} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb76f09-b098-57fa-b55a-31d6d25c86b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376008} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d51da6-f00d-568e-98e5-7a55230c872c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(-3-12t){e}^{5t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb76f09-b098-57fa-b55a-31d6d25c86b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376008} :answer-field/id #uuid "98223e8a-4da1-562e-9464-a4741a2867e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b306c145-fe13-5cb2-beb3-85a5684d2dfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-326239" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3432550f-6a81-568d-a5dd-8caa62692389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376012} {:answer/id #uuid "dbee9d91-fa37-5039-a76e-9577c64a1267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376013} {:answer/id #uuid "80261247-30e6-5796-9ac6-bea7d3eaac61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376014} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9b3dd4-8d7d-5f0a-9393-19fdce1d7e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376015} {:answer/id #uuid "7447cc5e-9277-5a27-9fe0-6427ca79613f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1+2t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80261247-30e6-5796-9ac6-bea7d3eaac61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376014} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb9217b-9477-55af-84ab-aad5da03331c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6790eea6-5ff2-5650-a1dc-b53741835848" :question/math-academy-id "q-80608" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1-1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2ea6e2-d62f-5ef1-b2c6-dcbd1521b55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376019} {:answer/id #uuid "67b0a269-de7c-5593-89cc-60c95b90a37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376020} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab3bca3-de72-575f-b516-588df7610ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376021} {:answer/id #uuid "12faa294-8415-5109-a808-c0b0d3604b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376022} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9735c1-dfe0-5c5d-b942-2ac227614d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d2ea6e2-d62f-5ef1-b2c6-dcbd1521b55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376019} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c3f93fa-f203-5435-8788-517cd2001ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8179a1bc-9dd2-5c9c-a7dd-3de3a7cd250c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80917" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdf1c37c-10a4-5e2a-949f-5cd3eff2dd34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376026} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd0fbb5-1bcb-5dd0-9670-531d53a413e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376027} {:answer/id #uuid "37ec5ecb-a790-5cdc-a966-9fc2d34d749e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376028} {:answer/id #uuid "8355817a-405f-51e7-ab2e-bd44687ff66a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376029} {:answer/id #uuid "91fc3af7-f641-56d8-9460-7f0e59ce4e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37ec5ecb-a790-5cdc-a966-9fc2d34d749e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376028} :answer-field/id #uuid "65331b9b-0fe1-556a-b17d-918abc8f8cfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f541f12-2ad2-5e8f-b9d5-4ff503431feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80918" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =5.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7-5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b6046c-093d-558b-91eb-3d5dd379068a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376033} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc40d3a-6536-592c-a0d4-a25315182155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376034} {:answer/id #uuid "fafdd1ca-bbc8-5c20-80b2-179694e2db86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376035} {:answer/id #uuid "ea123ce4-3a36-5d0f-88f0-d6bab0eef411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376036} {:answer/id #uuid "75bd4a95-3076-5928-bec7-0075105db27f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbc40d3a-6536-592c-a0d4-a25315182155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376034} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d2f6c9-49da-5726-bb1e-b95da2abe7dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bd476b7-be13-5d1b-9c4d-aad2517551db" :question/math-academy-id "q-80919" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1+1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1+1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}]}
